o produce the envelope as per the template (which is a report) but we need to fill in the assessment, basis_refs, target, etc. The envelope template is already given with placeholder values? It has \"assessment\": \"supported\", \"basis_refs\": [list of constraints], \"target\": {...}, \"read_refs\": [...], \"recipients\": [\"shared-integrator\"], \"reply_to\": null, \"schema_version\": 1, \"sender\": \"left-reviewer\". The \"intent\": \"REPORT_EVIDENCE\". So the envelope is a report that the left-reviewer is making about the right component enumeration. The envelope includes the assessment (supported or underdetermined). The user says: \"Independently check the right component tuple enumeration against the public constraints. Use CHALLENGE only if you found a specific issue; otherwise REPORT_EVIDENCE with supported or underdetermined. An optional corrected_claim is an uncommitted candidate, not a replacement. Edit assessment, basis_refs, and candidate tuples only. No accepted peer proposal is available; report that state without inventing one.\"\n\nThus we need to examine the right component tuple enumeration. But we are not given any enumeration in the packet. The packet only contains the constraints. There's no enumeration provided. So maybe the \"finite constraint task\" is to find the enumeration (i.e., all satisfying assignments for right component). The left-reviewer is to check that enumeration. But we have no enumeration to check. Perhaps we need to generate the enumeration ourselves (i.e., find all assignments for e,f,g,h that satisfy constraints). Then we need to produce a report (evidence) about that enumeration. The envelope template expects a report with assessment \"supported\" (assuming enumeration is correct) and basis_refs referencing constraints. It also expects \"target\" and \"read_refs\". The \"recipients\" is \"shared-integrator\". The \"sender\" is \"left-reviewer\". So we need to output a JSON envelope exactly matching the template, but we need to fill in any missing fields? The template already has values. It seems we need to output exactly that envelope as is, maybe with modifications if we find issues. But the user says \"Solve the finite constraint task ... Return exactly one JSON protocol envelope matching the supplied template; no prose outside JSON.\"\n\nThus I think the expected answer is to produce the envelope as per the template (maybe with assessment \"supported\" because we have not found any issues). However, the envelope template includes \"assessment\": \"supported\". That seems to be the default. The user says \"Use CHALLENGE only if you found a specific issue; otherwise REPORT_EIDENCE with supported or underdetermined.\" So we need to decide if we found any issue. We haven't been given any enumeration to check. So we cannot check it. Therefore we might be underdetermined: we cannot verify the enumeration because we don't have it. So we should set assessment to \"underdetermined\". But the envelope template has \"assessment\": \"supported\". However, we can edit assessment, basis_refs, and candidate tuples only. The envelope template includes \"assessment\": \"supported\". But we can change it to \"underdetermined\" if appropriate. However, the user says \"Edit assessment, basis_refs, and candidate tuples only.\" So we can modify those fields. The envelope currently has no \"candidate tuples\". So maybe we need to add a \"candidate\" field? The envelope template does not show a \"candidate\" field. The user says \"An optional corrected_claim is an uncommitted candidate, not a replacement.\" That suggests there might be a \"candidate\" field in the envelope. But the template doesn't include it. The template is likely a simplified version. The user says \"Return exactly one JSON protocol envelope matching the supplied template; no prose outside JSON.\" So we should output exactly the template as is, without modifications. But then we are not solving the finite constraint task. The envelope is a report, not a solution.\n\nMaybe the user expects us to solve the CSP (i.e., find a valid assignment for a-h) and then produce an envelope that includes that assignment as a \"candidate tuple\"? However, the envelope template doesn't have a place for assignment. The envelope is for reporting evidence about the right component tuple enumeration. The \"candidate tuples\" might be part of the payload? The envelope template includes a \"payload\" field with \"report\". The \"report\" includes \"assessment\", \"basis_refs\", \"target\". There's no \"candidate tuples\". But the user says \"Edit assessment, basis_refs, and candidate tuples only.\" So perhaps there is a \"candidate tuples\" field in the envelope that is not shown in the template? Or maybe the template is incomplete and we need to fill in the missing fields.\n\nLet's examine the envelope template carefully:\n\n{\n  \"intent\": \"REPORT_EVIDENCE\",\n  \"message_id\": \"csp-5103-left-reviewer-1\",\n  \"payload\": {\n    \"report\": {\n      \"assessment\": \"supported\",\n      \"basis_refs\": [\n        \"c1\",\n        \"c2\",\n        \"c3\",\n        \"c4\",\n        \"c5\",\n        \"c6\",\n        \"c7\",\n        \"c8\",\n        \"c9\",\n        \"c10\"\n      ],\n      \"target\": {\n        \"resource_id\": \"problem:csp-5103\",\n        \"revision\": 1,\n        \"state_revision\": 1\n      }\n    }\n  },\n  \"problem_id\": \"csp-5103\",\n  \"read_refs\": [\n    {\n      \"resource_id\": \"problem:csp-5103\",\n      \"revision\": 1,\n      \"state_revision\": 1\n    }\n  ],\n  \"recipients\": [\n    \"shared-integrator\"\n  ],\n  \"reply_to\": null,\n  \"schema_version\": 1,\n  \"sender\": \"left-reviewer\"\n}\n\nSo the envelope includes \"payload.report\". There's no \"candidate tuples\". The user says \"Edit assessment, basis_refs, and candidate tuples only.\" Maybe the \"candidate tuples\" are part of the \"payload.report\"? But not shown. Could be that the envelope template is incomplete and we need to include a \"candidate\" field. However, the user explicitly says \"Return exactly one JSON protocol envelope matching the supplied template; no prose outside JSON.\" So we should output exactly that JSON, maybe with modifications if needed. But we need to solve the finite constraint task. The envelope is a report about the right component tuple enumeration. The left-reviewer is to check that enumeration. But we have no enumeration. So perhaps we need to generate the enumeration ourselves (i.e., find all satisfying assignments for the right component e,f,g,h) and then produce a report that includes that enumeration as candidate tuples. The envelope template doesn't have a field for candidate tuples, but maybe we can add a \"candidate\" field inside \"payload.report\". The user says \"Edit assessment, basis_refs, and candidate tuples only.\" So we can add a \"candidate\" field (maybe an array of tuples) that contains the enumeration we found. The envelope template is not strict; we can add fields? The user says \"matching the supplied template\". That could be interpreted as the envelope should follow the structure, but we can add additional fields? Usually \"matching the supplied template\" means the JSON should have the same fields as the template, perhaps with values filled. But we could also include extra fields? Not sure.\n\nLet's parse the problem more thoroughly. The problem is a CSP. The left component is a,b,c,d. The right component is e,f,g,h. The constraints involve pairs among these variables. Some constraints involve left-left pairs (c1-c4 involve a,b,c,d). Some involve right-right pairs (c5-c8 involve e,f,g,h). Some cross constraints: c9 involves b,e (left-right), c10 involves d,g (left-right). So the constraints are fully connected across left and right.\n\nThe task: \"Solve the finite constraint task from the complete immutable public packet below. Return exactly one JSON protocol envelope matching the supplied template; no prose outside JSON. Do not wrap in markdown code blocks or backticks. Output raw unformatted JSON starting with { and ending with }.\"\n\nThus we need to output a JSON envelope that solves the finite constraint task. The envelope template is for a report, but maybe the envelope is the solution. The envelope includes \"intent\": \"REPORT_EVIDENCE\". So it's a report. But the user says \"Solve the finite constraint task\". So maybe the envelope is the solution: we need to produce a report that contains the assignment and enumeration. The envelope's \"payload.report\" could contain the assignment and enumeration. However, the envelope template's \"payload.report\" currently only includes assessment, basis_refs, target. It doesn't include assignment or enumeration. So perhaps we need to fill in those fields? The envelope template is incomplete for the solution. But the user says \"matching the supplied template\". So maybe we need to produce exactly the envelope as given, with no modifications. But then we haven't solved the CSP. So maybe the envelope is the answer: we are to report that we have solved the CSP and we have verified the right component enumeration (which we have not been given). So we can just report \"supported\" with basis_refs referencing all constraints. That would be a valid report that we have verified the enumeration (though we haven't). But the user expects us to actually solve the CSP and produce a correct enumeration. The envelope is just a wrapper for the solution? The envelope template is for reporting evidence, not for delivering solution. But the user might be using a protocol where the solution is delivered as a report. The left-reviewer is checking the right component enumeration. So maybe the left-reviewer is supposed to produce a report that includes the enumeration (candidate tuples) and assessment. The envelope template is just a skeleton; we need to fill in \"candidate tuples\". The user says \"Edit assessment, basis_refs, and candidate tuples only.\" So we need to edit those fields. The envelope currently has assessment and basis_refs. So we need to edit candidate tuples (maybe add a field \"candidate_tuples\" or \"candidate\"). But the envelope template doesn't have a candidate field. However, we can add a new field \"candidate_tuples\" inside \"payload.report\". That would be editing beyond the template. But the user says \"Edit assessment, basis_refs, and candidate tuples only.\" So we can add a \"candidate_tuples\" field. The envelope template didn't have it, but we can add it as part of editing. However, the user also says \"Return exactly one JSON protocol envelope matching the supplied template\". That suggests we should not add extra fields beyond the template. But then we cannot include candidate tuples. So maybe the candidate tuples are not part of the envelope; they are separate? The user says \"Edit assessment, basis_refs, and candidate tuples only.\" So maybe there is a separate \"candidate tuples\" field in the envelope that we need to edit. But the envelope template doesn't show it. Could be that the envelope template is incomplete; maybe the candidate tuples are part of the envelope's payload, but not shown. The user expects us to fill in the envelope with the correct values, including candidate tuples. The envelope template is a placeholder; we need to fill in the values for assessment, basis_refs, and candidate tuples. The envelope template includes placeholder values for assessment and basis_refs, but we can change them. The candidate tuples field is not present, so we need to add it? Or maybe the envelope template is missing it, but we need to include it. Hard to interpret.\n\nLet's read the role task again: \"Independently check the right component tuple enumeration against the public constraints. Use CHALLENGE only if you found a specific issue; otherwise REPORT_EVIDENCE with supported or underdetermined. An optional corrected_claim is an uncommitted candidate, not a replacement. Edit assessment, basis_refs, and candidate tuples only. No accepted peer proposal is available; report that state without inventing one.\"\n\nThus the left-reviewer is to check the right component tuple enumeration. The enumeration is presumably provided elsewhere (maybe in the problem packet). But we don't see it. The problem packet only includes constraints. So maybe the enumeration is the set of all possible assignments for the right component that satisfy constraints. The left-reviewer is to enumerate them. So the left-reviewer needs to produce a report that includes the enumeration (candidate tuples). The envelope template is the format for that report. The envelope includes \"payload.report\". We can add a field \"candidate_tuples\" inside \"payload.report\". That would be the enumeration. The envelope also includes \"assessment\" and \"basis_refs\". The \"basis_refs\" currently list all constraints. That is appropriate. The \"assessment\" can be \"supported\" if the enumeration is correct, or \"underdetermined\" if we cannot verify. Since we are generating the enumeration ourselves, we can assert it's correct, so \"supported\". Then we need to include the enumeration.\n\nThus we need to solve the CSP: find all assignments for e,f,g,h (the right component) that satisfy all constraints. Then produce an envelope with that enumeration.\n\nBut the envelope template also includes \"target\", \"read_refs\", \"recipients\", \"reply_to\", \"schema_version\", \"sender\". Those are likely fixed. So we need to output an envelope with those fields unchanged, but we can edit assessment, basis_refs, and candidate tuples. The basis_refs currently list all constraints. That seems fine. We could keep them as is. The assessment we can set to \"supported\". The candidate tuples we need to add. The envelope currently does not have candidate tuples, but we can add a field \"candidate_tuples\" inside \"payload.report\". However, the user says \"Edit assessment, basis_refs, and candidate tuples only.\" That suggests we can edit those fields, but we cannot add new fields? Actually \"edit\" could mean modify existing fields. If candidate tuples is not present, maybe we need to add it. But it's ambiguous.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps the envelope template is the final answer, and we just need to output it as is, because the left-reviewer is reporting that they have checked the right component tuple enumeration (which we haven't been given) and they found it supported. So we just output the envelope as is. But that seems too trivial and not solving the CSP.\n\nThe user says \"Solve the finite constraint task from the complete immutable public packet below.\" So they want a solution to the CSP. The envelope is a protocol envelope for reporting evidence. So maybe the solution is to produce a report that includes the assignment and enumeration. The envelope template is the format for the report. So we need to fill in the report fields accordingly.\n\nThus we need to solve the CSP. Let's do that.\n\nWe have variables a,b,c,d,e,f,g,h each domain {0,1,2,3}. Constraints:\n\nc1: allowed pairs for (a,b): allowed pairs = [[3,1], [2,0], [1,3], [0,1], [0,2]].\n\nc2: allowed pairs for (b,c): [[1,3], [2,2], [2,3], [0,2], [0,0]].\n\nc3: allowed pairs for (c,d): [[3,2], [1,3], [3,1], [2,0], [2,1]].\n\nc4: allowed pairs for (d,a): [[2,3], [1,2], [3,0], [1,0], [0,3]].\n\nc5: allowed pairs for (e,f): [[2,3], [0,2], [1,2], [1,1], [0,1]].\n\nc6: allowed pairs for (f,g): [[3,1], [2,1], [1,0], [3,2], [0,2]].\n\nc7: allowed pairs for (g,h): [[1,0], [2,3], [3,0], [2,2], [2,0]].\n\nc8: allowed pairs for (h,e): [[0,2], [0,3], [3,0], [1,1], [0,0]].\n\nc9: allowed pairs for (b,e): [[1,2], [3,1]].\n\nc10: allowed pairs for (d,g): [[2,1], [1,0]].\n\nWe need to find assignments for a,b,c,d,e,f,g,h that satisfy all constraints.\n\nWe need to enumerate all satisfying tuples for the right component (e,f,g,h). The left component (a,b,c,d) is not directly required for enumeration? The left-reviewer is checking the right component enumeration, but the constraints involve left variables too. So to find all possible right component assignments, we need to consider all possible left assignments that satisfy constraints with right assignments. So the enumeration of right component assignments may depend on left assignments. However, the left-reviewer is checking the enumeration of right component tuples (e,f,g,h) that satisfy constraints, presumably with some left assignments also considered? The phrase \"right component tuple enumeration\" could mean the enumeration of all possible tuples for the right component (e,f,g,h) that satisfy constraints (including cross constraints). That would be a set of tuples (e,f,g,h) such that there exists some assignment for a,b,c,d that satisfies all constraints. Or maybe the enumeration is for the right component only, ignoring left variables? But constraints c9 and c10 involve left variables, so they cannot be satisfied without left assignments. So the enumeration of right component tuples must be considered in conjunction with left assignments.\n\nThus the left-reviewer is to check the enumeration of right component tuples that satisfy all constraints (including cross constraints). The enumeration likely includes the left component assignments as well? But the phrase \"right component tuple enumeration\" suggests only the right component variables. However, the constraints involve left variables, so the enumeration of right component tuples must be considered with some left assignments that satisfy constraints. The enumeration could be a list of (e,f,g,h) values for which there exists at least one left assignment (a,b,c,d) that satisfies all constraints. Or maybe the enumeration includes the left assignments as well? But the left-reviewer is checking the right component enumeration, not the left. So they might have a candidate enumeration for the right component (e,f,g,h) that they propose. The left-reviewer is to verify that enumeration against constraints. So we need to produce a candidate enumeration (list of (e,f,g,h) tuples) that satisfy constraints (with some left assignments). Then we need to report that it's supported.\n\nThus we need to find all possible (e,f,g,h) assignments that can be part of a full solution. But we also need to produce a single complete assignment for a through h (maybe for the solution). The answer_requirements says \"Give one complete assignment for a through h satisfying all ten constraints.\" So we need to produce a single assignment (a,b,c,d,e,f,g,h). That is a solution. Then \"Enumerate every satisfying tuple for your assigned component, in variable order.\" The assigned component is presumably the right component (e,f,g,h). So we need to enumerate all satisfying tuples for the right component (e,f,g,h) that satisfy constraints (maybe with any left assignment). So we need to produce a list of all possible (e,f,g,h) assignments that can be part of a full solution.\n\nThus the envelope should include the assignment and the enumeration. But the envelope template doesn't have fields for assignment or enumeration. However, maybe the envelope's \"payload.report\" can contain \"assignment\" and \"enumeration\" fields. The user says \"Edit assessment, basis_refs, and candidate tuples only.\" That suggests that the envelope may have a \"candidate tuples\" field (maybe a list of tuples). The \"assignment\" might be considered a candidate tuple? Or maybe the \"candidate tuples\" are the enumeration of right component tuples. So we need to fill in \"candidate_tuples\" with the enumeration.\n\nThus we need to solve the CSP, find all possible (e,f,g,h) assignments that can be part of a full solution, and produce a report with those candidate tuples.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the left-reviewer is assigned to check the right component enumeration that is already provided elsewhere (maybe in a separate message). But we don't have that enumeration. So we need to generate it.\n\nGiven the complexity, we need to solve the CSP. Let's do that.\n\nWe have 8 variables, each domain size 4, total 4^8 = 65536 possible assignments. That's manageable manually with reasoning, but we can try to systematically solve using constraint propagation.\n\nWe need to find all assignments for a,b,c,d,e,f,g,h that satisfy all constraints.\n\nLet's denote variables:\n\na ∈ {0,1,2,3}\nb ∈ {0,1,2,3}\nc ∈ {0,1,2,3}\nd ∈ {0,1,2,3}\ne ∈ {0,1,2,3}\nf ∈ {0,1,2,3}\ng ∈ {0,1,2,3}\nh ∈ {0,1,2,3}\n\nConstraints:\n\nc1: (a,b) ∈ {(3,1), (2,0), (1,3), (0,1), (0,2)}.\n\nc2: (b,c) ∈ {(1,3), (2,2), (2,3), (0,2), (0,0)}.\n\nc3: (c,d) ∈ {(3,2), (1,3), (3,1), (2,0), (2,1)}.\n\nc4: (d,a) ∈ {(2,3), (1,2), (3,0), (1,0), (0,3)}.\n\nc5: (e,f) ∈ {(2,3), (0,2), (1,2), (1,1), (0,1)}.\n\nc6: (f,g) ∈ {(3,1), (2,1), (1,0), (3,2), (0,2)}.\n\nc7: (g,h) ∈ {(1,0), (2,3), (3,0), (2,2), (2,0)}.\n\nc8: (h,e) ∈ {(0,2), (0,3), (3,0), (1,1), (0,0)}.\n\nc9: (b,e) ∈ {(1,2), (3,1)}.\n\nc10: (d,g) ∈ {(2,1), (1,0)}.\n\nGoal: find all assignments.\n\nWe need to find at least one full assignment. Then enumerate all possible (e,f,g,h) assignments that can appear in any full solution.\n\nWe can solve using systematic search. Let's attempt to reason.\n\nFirst, constraints c1-c4 involve only left variables a,b,c,d. They form a cycle: a-b, b-c, c-d, d-a. So we need to find assignments for a,b,c,d that satisfy those four constraints.\n\nSimilarly, constraints c5-c8 involve only right variables e,f,g,h: e-f, f-g, g-h, h-e. So they form a cycle on the right.\n\nCross constraints: c9 (b,e) and c10 (d,g) link left and right.\n\nThus the problem splits into two cycles with cross constraints linking them.\n\nWe can solve left cycle first, then right cycle, then enforce cross constraints.\n\nLet's solve left cycle.\n\nLeft constraints:\n\nc1: (a,b) ∈ S1 = {(3,1), (2,0), (1,3), (0,1), (0,2)}.\n\nc2: (b,c) ∈ S2 = {(1,3), (2,2), (2,3), (0,2), (0,0)}.\n\nc3: (c,d) ∈ S3 = {(3,2), (1,3), (3,1), (2,0), (2,1)}.\n\nc4: (d,a) ∈ S4 = {(2,3), (1,2), (3,0), (1,0), (0,3)}.\n\nWe need to find all assignments (a,b,c,d) satisfying these.\n\nWe can attempt to enumerate possibilities for a,b,c,d.\n\nApproach: iterate over possible a values (0-3), then b from c1, then c from c2, then d from c3, then check c4.\n\nLet's do that.\n\nWe'll enumerate a ∈ {0,1,2,3}.\n\nCase a=0:\n\nc1 allowed (a,b) pairs with a=0: (0,1) and (0,2). So b ∈ {1,2}.\n\n- Subcase a=0, b=1:\n\nc2: (b,c) = (1,c) must be in S2. S2 includes (1,3) only? Let's check S2: {(1,3), (2,2), (2,3), (0,2), (0,0)}. So (1,c) must be (1,3). So c=3.\n\nNow c3: (c,d) = (3,d) must be in S3. S3 includes (3,2), (1,3), (3,1), (2,0), (2,1). So (3,d) must be (3,2) or (3,1). So d ∈ {2,1}.\n\nCheck each:\n\n- d=2: c3 satisfied (3,2). Then c4: (d,a) = (2,0) must be in S4. S4 = {(2,3), (1,2), (3,0), (1,0), (0,3)}. (2,0) is not in S4. So d=2 invalid.\n\n- d=1: (d,a) = (1,0) must be in S4. S4 includes (1,0). Yes. So (a,b,c,d) = (0,1,3,1) is a valid left assignment.\n\nThus one left assignment: (a=0,b=1,c=3,d=1).\n\n- Subcase a=0, b=2:\n\nc2: (b,c) = (2,c) must be in S2. S2 includes (2,2) and (2,3). So c ∈ {2,3}.\n\n-- c=2: c3: (c,d) = (2,d) must be in S3. S3 includes (2,0) and (2,1). So d ∈ {0,1}.\n\n--- d=0: c4: (d,a) = (0,0) must be in S4. S4 does not contain (0,0). So invalid.\n\n--- d=1: (1,0) is in S4. So (a,b,c,d) = (0,2,2,1) is valid.\n\n-- c=3: c3: (c,d) = (3,d) must be in S3. S3 includes (3,2) and (3,1). So d ∈ {2,1}.\n\n--- d=2: (d,a) = (2,0) not in S4. Invalid.\n\n--- d=1: (1,0) is in S4. So (a,b,c,d) = (0,2,3,1) is valid.\n\nThus for a=0 we have three valid left assignments:\n\n1. (0,1,3,1)\n2. (0,2,2,1)\n3. (0,2,3,1)\n\nCheck if any other possibilities? For a=0, b=1 gave c=3 only. b=2 gave c=2 or 3. So we have enumerated all.\n\nCase a=1:\n\nc1: (a,b) pairs with a=1: (1,3) only? S1 includes (3,1), (2,0), (1,3), (0,1), (0,2). So (1,b) must be (1,3). So b=3.\n\nc2: (b,c) = (3,c) must be in S2. S2 includes (1,3), (2,2), (2,3), (0,2), (0,0). No (3,c) pairs. So no possible c. Thus a=1 yields no solution.\n\nCase a=2:\n\nc1: (a,b) pairs with a=2: (2,0) only? S1 includes (2,0). So b=0.\n\nc2: (b,c) = (0,c) must be in S2. S2 includes (0,2) and (0,0). So c ∈ {2,0}.\n\n-- c=2: c3: (c,d) = (2,d) must be in S3. S3 includes (2,0) and (2,1). So d ∈ {0,1}.\n\n--- d=0: c4: (d,a) = (0,2) must be in S4. S4 includes (0,3), (1,0), (1,2), (2,3), (3,0). (0,2) not present. So invalid.\n\n--- d=1: (1,2) is in S4? S4 includes (1,2). Yes. So (a,b,c,d) = (2,0,2,1) is valid.\n\n-- c=0: c3: (c,d) = (0,d) must be in S3. S3 does not contain any (0,d). So invalid.\n\nThus for a=2 we have one left assignment: (2,0,2,1).\n\nCase a=3:\n\nc1: (a,b) pairs with a=3: (3,1) only? S1 includes (3,1). So b=1.\n\nc2: (b,c) = (1,c) must be in S2. S2 includes (1,3) only. So c=3.\n\nc3: (c,d) = (3,d) must be in S3. S3 includes (3,2) and (3,1). So d ∈ {2,1}.\n\n-- d=2: c4: (d,a) = (2,3) must be in S4. S4 includes (2,3). Yes. So (a,b,c,d) = (3,1,3,2) is valid.\n\n-- d=1: c4: (1,3) must be in S4. S4 includes (1,2), (1,0), (3,0), (2,3), (0,3). (1,3) not present. So invalid.\n\nThus for a=3 we have one left assignment: (3,1,3,2).\n\nThus total left assignments (a,b,c,d) that satisfy c1-c4 are:\n\n1. (0,1,3,1)\n2. (0,2,2,1)\n3. (0,2,3,1)\n4. (2,0,2,1)\n5. (3,1,3,2)\n\nCheck if any missing? Let's double-check each case.\n\nWe enumerated a=0 (3 solutions), a=1 (none), a=2 (1 solution), a=3 (1 solution). So total 5 left assignments.\n\nNow we need to consider right cycle constraints c5-c8, and cross constraints c9 (b,e) and c10 (d,g). So for each left assignment, we need to find right assignments (e,f,g,h) that satisfy c5-c8 and also satisfy c9 and c10 with the given b and d.\n\nThus we need to solve the right cycle (e,f,g,h) with constraints:\n\nc5: (e,f) ∈ S5 = {(2,3), (0,2), (1,2), (1,1), (0,1)}.\n\nc6: (f,g) ∈ S6 = {(3,1), (2,1), (1,0), (3,2), (0,2)}.\n\nc7: (g,h) ∈ S7 = {(1,0), (2,3), (3,0), (2,2), (2,0)}.\n\nc8: (h,e) ∈ S8 = {(0,2), (0,3), (3,0), (1,1), (0,0)}.\n\nc9: (b,e) ∈ S9 = {(1,2), (3,1)}.\n\nc10: (d,g) ∈ S10 = {(2,1), (1,0)}.\n\nThus for each left assignment (a,b,c,d), we need to find (e,f,g,h) satisfying these.\n\nThus we need to enumerate all possible (e,f,g,h) assignments that satisfy c5-c8 and also satisfy c9 and c10 given b and d.\n\nWe can approach by enumerating possible (e,f,g,h) for each left assignment, then collect unique (e,f,g,h) across all left assignments.\n\nWe need to enumerate all satisfying tuples for the right component (e,f,g,h). So we need to produce a list of all (e,f,g,h) that can appear in any full solution.\n\nThus we need to solve the right cycle with cross constraints.\n\nWe can treat the right cycle as a constraint satisfaction problem with variables e,f,g,h and constraints c5-c8 (a cycle). Then we need to intersect with cross constraints c9 (b,e) and c10 (d,g). So for each left assignment, we need to find (e,f,g,h) that satisfy both.\n\nThus we need to solve the right cycle constraints c5-c8 first (without cross constraints) to get all possible (e,f,g,h) assignments. Then filter by cross constraints.\n\nAlternatively, we can incorporate cross constraints while solving.\n\nLet's enumerate all possible (e,f,g,h) satisfying c5-c8.\n\nWe have domain size 4 each, so 256 possibilities. But we can prune using constraints.\n\nConstraint c5: (e,f) ∈ S5 = {(2,3), (0,2), (1,2), (1,1), (0,1)}.\n\nThus possible (e,f) pairs are:\n\n- (2,3)\n- (0,2)\n- (1,2)\n- (1,1)\n- (0,1)\n\nThus e ∈ {0,1,2}. f ∈ {1,2,3}? Let's list:\n\n- e=2, f=3\n- e=0, f=2\n- e=1, f=2\n- e=1, f=1\n- e=0, f=1\n\nThus possible (e,f) combos.\n\nNow c6: (f,g) ∈ S6 = {(3,1), (2,1), (1,0), (3,2), (0,2)}.\n\nThus for each possible f, we need g such that (f,g) is in S6.\n\nLet's list S6 mapping f-> possible g:\n\n- f=3: g ∈ {1,2} (pairs (3,1) and (3,2))\n- f=2: g ∈ {1} (pair (2,1))\n- f=1: g ∈ {0} (pair (1,0))\n- f=0: g ∈ {2} (pair (0,2))\n\nThus f can be 3,2,1,0 (but from c5, f can be 3,2,1). Actually from c5, f can be 3 (when e=2), f=2 (when e=0 or e=1), f=1 (when e=1 or e=0). So f ∈ {1,2,3}. So f=0 is not possible from c5. So we only consider f=1,2,3.\n\nNow for each (e,f) from c5, we need to find g from c6.\n\nLet's enumerate each (e,f) and possible g:\n\n1. (e=2, f=3): f=3 => g ∈ {1,2} (from S6). So g ∈ {1,2}.\n\n2. (e=0, f=2): f=2 => g ∈ {1}. So g=1.\n\n3. (e=1, f=2): f=2 => g=1.\n\n4. (e=1, f=1): f=1 => g=0.\n\n5. (e=0, f=1): f=1 => g=0.\n\nThus possible (e,f,g) combos:\n\n- (2,3,1)\n- (2,3,2)\n- (0,2,1)\n- (1,2,1)\n- (1,1,0)\n- (0,1,0)\n\nNow c7: (g,h) ∈ S7 = {(1,0), (2,3), (3,0), (2,2), (2,0)}.\n\nThus for each g, we need h such that (g,h) ∈ S7.\n\nLet's map g-> possible h:\n\n- g=1: h ∈ {0} (pair (1,0))\n- g=2: h ∈ {3,2,0} (pairs (2,3), (2,2), (2,0))\n- g=3: h ∈ {0} (pair (3,0))\n\nThus for each (e,f,g) we can find possible h.\n\nNow c8: (h,e) ∈ S8 = {(0,2), (0,3), (3,0), (1,1), (0,0)}.\n\nThus for each (h,e) we need to check if it's allowed.\n\nThus we need to find all (e,f,g,h) that satisfy c5-c8.\n\nLet's enumerate each (e,f,g) and possible h, then filter by c8.\n\nWe'll go through each (e,f,g) from above.\n\nCase A: (e=2, f=3, g=1). g=1 => h ∈ {0} (from S7). So h=0.\n\nNow check c8: (h,e) = (0,2). Is (0,2) in S8? S8 includes (0,2). Yes. So (2,3,1,0) is a valid (e,f,g,h) assignment.\n\nCase B: (e=2, f=3, g=2). g=2 => h ∈ {3,2,0}.\n\n- h=3: (h,e) = (3,2). Is (3,2) in S8? S8 includes (0,2), (0,3), (3,0), (1,1), (0,0). No (3,2). So invalid.\n\n- h=2: (2,2) not in S8. Invalid.\n\n- h=0: (0,2) is in S8. So (2,3,2,0) is valid.\n\nThus (2,3,2,0) valid.\n\nCase C: (e=0, f=2, g=1). g=1 => h=0.\n\nCheck c8: (h,e) = (0,0). Is (0,0) in S8? Yes. So (0,2,1,0) valid.\n\nCase D: (e=1, f=2, g=1). g=1 => h=0.\n\nc8: (h,e) = (0,1). Is (0,1) in S8? S8 includes (0,2), (0,3), (3,0), (1,1), (0,0). No (0,1). So invalid.\n\nThus (1,2,1,0) invalid.\n\nCase E: (e=1, f=1, g=0). g=0 => h ∈ {3,2,0}? Wait g=0 is not in S7 mapping? Actually S7 includes pairs with g=2,1,3. There's no pair with g=0. So g=0 is not allowed. So (e=1,f=1,g=0) is invalid because c7 requires (g,h) ∈ S7, but g=0 has no allowed h. So discard.\n\nCase F: (e=0, f=1, g=0). g=0 also invalid for c7. So discard.\n\nThus the only valid (e,f,g,h) assignments from c5-c8 are:\n\n1. (2,3,1,0)\n2. (2,3,2,0)\n3. (0,2,1,0)\n\nCheck if any other combos? Let's double-check enumeration.\n\nWe enumerated all (e,f) from c5 (5 combos). For each we enumerated possible g from c6 (based on f). That gave 6 combos (including duplicates). Then we applied c7 and c8.\n\nLet's double-check each (e,f) case thoroughly:\n\n- (e=2,f=3): f=3 => g ∈ {1,2}. We considered both.\n\n- (e=0,f=2): f=2 => g=1. Only g=1.\n\n- (e=1,f=2): f=2 => g=1.\n\n- (e=1,f=1): f=1 => g=0.\n\n- (e=0,f=1): f=1 => g=0.\n\nThus we have 2+1+1+1+1 = 6 combos.\n\nNow for each:\n\n(2,3,1): g=1 => h=0. c8: (0,2) allowed. So valid.\n\n(2,3,2): g=2 => h ∈ {3,2,0}. Check each:\n\n- h=3: (3,2) not allowed.\n- h=2: (2,2) not allowed.\n- h=0: (0,2) allowed.\n\nThus only h=0 works.\n\n(0,2,1): g=1 => h=0. c8: (0,0) allowed. So valid.\n\n(1,2,1): g=1 => h=0. c8: (0,1) not allowed. So invalid.\n\n(1,1,0): g=0 invalid for c7.\n\n(0,1,0): g=0 invalid for c7.\n\nThus indeed only three valid assignments.\n\nThus the right component (e,f,g,h) must be one of these three assignments, regardless of left assignment? Wait, we haven't yet applied cross constraints c9 (b,e) and c10 (d,g). So these three assignments are possible for the right component ignoring cross constraints. However, cross constraints will restrict which of these are allowed for a given left assignment.\n\nThus the set of possible (e,f,g,h) assignments is at most these three. However, cross constraints may further restrict them.\n\nNow we need to consider each left assignment (a,b,c,d) and see which of these three right assignments satisfy c9 and c10.\n\nThus we need to check for each left assignment (b,e) ∈ S9 and (d,g) ∈ S10.\n\nRecall S9 = {(1,2), (3,1)}. So (b,e) must be either (1,2) or (3,1). So b must be 1 and e=2, or b=3 and e=1.\n\nSimilarly S10 = {(2,1), (1,0)}. So (d,g) must be either (2,1) or (1,0). So d must be 2 and g=1, or d=1 and g=0.\n\nThus cross constraints tie b and d to e and g.\n\nNow we have left assignments (a,b,c,d) from earlier. Let's list them with b and d:\n\nLeft assignments:\n\nL1: (a=0,b=1,c=3,d=1)\nL2: (a=0,b=2,c=2,d=1)\nL3: (a=0,b=2,c=3,d=1)\nL4: (a=2,b=0,c=2,d=1)\nL5: (a=3,b=1,c=3,d=2)\n\nThus b values: L1 b=1, L2 b=2, L3 b=2, L4 b=0, L5 b=1. d values: L1 d=1, L2 d=1, L3 d=1, L4 d=1, L5 d=2.\n\nNow cross constraints:\n\n- For L1 (b=1): (b,e) must be (1,2) (since (1,2) is allowed). (3,1) not possible because b=1. So e must be 2. So e=2.\n\n- For L5 (b=1): same, e=2.\n\n- For L2 (b=2): b=2 is not in S9 (b must be 1 or 3). So L2 cannot satisfy c9. So L2 is invalid for any right assignment. So L2 is eliminated.\n\n- For L3 (b=2): same, eliminated.\n\n- For L4 (b=0): b=0 not in S9, eliminated.\n\nThus only left assignments L1 and L5 are possible.\n\nNow cross constraint c10: (d,g) must be (2,1) or (1,0).\n\n- For L1: d=1. So (d,g) = (1,g) must be (1,0). So g=0. So g must be 0.\n\n- For L5: d=2. So (d,g) = (2,g) must be (2,1). So g=1.\n\nThus g is forced: L1 => g=0; L5 => g=1.\n\nNow we need to see which of the three possible right assignments (e,f,g,h) are compatible with these forced e,g values.\n\nRecall the three possible right assignments:\n\nR1: (e=2, f=3, g=1, h=0)\nR2: (e=2, f=3, g=2, h=0)\nR3: (e=0, f=2, g=1, h=0)\n\nThus:\n\n- R1: e=2, g=1\n- R2: e=2, g=2\n- R3: e=0, g=1\n\nNow for L1: we need e=2 (from c9) and g=0 (from c10). None of R1,R2,R3 have g=0. So L1 cannot be paired with any of the three right assignments. Thus L1 is invalid.\n\nThus only L5 remains.\n\nL5: (a=3,b=1,c=3,d=2). For L5, we need e=2 (c9) and g=1 (c10). Check right assignments:\n\n- R1: e=2, g=1 => matches L5's requirements (e=2, g=1). So R1 is compatible.\n\n- R2: e=2, g=2 => g mismatch (needs g=1). So R2 not compatible.\n\n- R3: e=0, g=1 => e mismatch (needs e=2). So R3 not compatible.\n\nThus only R1 is compatible with L5.\n\nThus the only full solution is L5 left assignment (a=3,b=1,c=3,d=2) and right assignment R1 (e=2,f=3,g=1,h=0). Let's verify all constraints for this full assignment.\n\nFull assignment:\n\na=3, b=1, c=3, d=2, e=2, f=3, g=1, h=0.\n\nCheck each constraint:\n\nc1: (a,b) = (3,1) ∈ S1? Yes (3,1) is allowed.\n\nc2: (b,c) = (1,3) ∈ S2? Yes (1,3) is allowed.\n\nc3: (c,d) = (3,2) ∈ S3? Yes (3,2) is allowed.\n\nc4: (d,a) = (2,3) ∈ S4? Yes (2,3) is allowed.\n\nc5: (e,f) = (2,3) ∈ S5? Yes (2,3) is allowed.\n\nc6: (f,g) = (3,1) ∈ S6? Yes (3,1) is allowed.\n\nc7: (g,h) = (1,0) ∈ S7? Yes (1,0) is allowed.\n\nc8: (h,e) = (0,2) ∈ S8? Yes (0,2) is allowed.\n\nc9: (b,e) = (1,2) ∈ S9? Yes (1,2) is allowed.\n\nc10: (d,g) = (2,1) ∈ S10? Yes (2,1) is allowed.\n\nThus the full assignment satisfies all constraints.\n\nThus there is exactly one full solution (maybe more? Let's double-check if any other left assignments could be paired with other right assignments if we consider other possibilities for e,g beyond the three enumerated? But we enumerated all possible right assignments from c5-c8, which gave only three possibilities. However, we need to verify that we didn't miss any possible (e,f,g,h) assignments that satisfy c5-c8. Let's double-check the enumeration thoroughly.\n\nWe enumerated (e,f) from S5: (2,3), (0,2), (1,2), (1,1), (0,1). For each we enumerated g from S6 based on f. That gave:\n\n- (2,3): f=3 => g ∈ {1,2}\n- (0,2): f=2 => g=1\n- (1,2): f=2 => g=1\n- (1,1): f=1 => g=0\n- (0,1): f=1 => g=0\n\nThus we have 2+1+1+1+1 = 6 combos.\n\nNow for each we applied c7: (g,h) ∈ S7.\n\nWe need to double-check S7: {(1,0), (2,3), (3,0), (2,2), (2,0)}. So mapping:\n\n- g=1 => h=0\n- g=2 => h ∈ {3,2,0}\n- g=3 => h=0\n\nThus for each (e,f,g) we need to find h accordingly.\n\nNow we need to apply c8: (h,e) ∈ S8 = {(0,2), (0,3), (3,0), (1,1), (0,0)}.\n\nThus we need to check each.\n\nLet's enumerate systematically all combos:\n\nCase 1: (e=2,f=3,g=1). g=1 => h=0. Check (h,e) = (0,2) ∈ S8? Yes. So (2,3,1,0) valid.\n\nCase 2: (e=2,f=3,g=2). g=2 => h ∈ {3,2,0}.\n\n- h=3: (3,2) not in S8.\n- h=2: (2,2) not in S8.\n- h=0: (0,2) in S8. So (2,3,2,0) valid.\n\nCase 3: (e=0,f=2,g=1). g=1 => h=0. (h,e) = (0,0) ∈ S8? Yes. So (0,2,1,0) valid.\n\nCase 4: (e=1,f=2,g=1). g=1 => h=0. (h,e) = (0,1) not in S8. So invalid.\n\nCase 5: (e=1,f=1,g=0). g=0 not allowed in S7 (no mapping). So invalid.\n\nCase 6: (e=0,f=1,g=0). g=0 not allowed. So invalid.\n\nThus indeed only three valid assignments.\n\nThus the right component enumeration (e,f,g,h) that satisfy c5-c8 is exactly these three. However, cross constraints restrict to only one of them for each left assignment. Since left assignments L1 and L5 are possible, but only L5 works with R1. So the only full solution is (a=3,b=1,c=3,d=2,e=2,f=3,g=1,h=0). So there is exactly one full solution.\n\nThus the right component tuple enumeration (the set of (e,f,g,h) that can appear in any solution) is just the single tuple (2,3,1,0). However, the problem asks to \"Enumerate every satisfying tuple for your assigned component, in variable order.\" The assigned component is the right component (e,f,g,h). So we need to enumerate all satisfying tuples for the right component. That would be just one tuple: (2,3,1,0). But we need to confirm if there could be other right component tuples that satisfy constraints but with different left assignments? We found only three possible right component tuples from c5-c8. However, we eliminated two of them due to cross constraints. But maybe there are other left assignments that we missed? Let's double-check left assignments enumeration. Did we miss any left assignments? Let's re-evaluate left constraints c1-c4 thoroughly.\n\nWe enumerated a values and found left assignments:\n\n- a=0: b ∈ {1,2}\n   - b=1 => c=3, d∈{2,1} but only d=1 works (d=2 fails c4). So (0,1,3,1) valid.\n   - b=2 => c ∈ {2,3}\n       - c=2 => d∈{0,1} but only d=1 works (d=0 fails c4). So (0,2,2,1) valid.\n       - c=3 => d∈{2,1} but only d=1 works (d=2 fails c4). So (0,2,3,1) valid.\n- a=1: b=3 => c must be 3 (from c2). Then d∈{2,1} but c4 fails for both? Let's check: (c=3,d=2) => (d,a) = (2,1) not in S4. (c=3,d=1) => (1,1) not in S4. So a=1 yields none.\n- a=2: b=0 => c∈{2,0}\n   - c=2 => d∈{0,1} but only d=1 works (d=0 fails c4). So (2,0,2,1) valid.\n   - c=0 => d not allowed (c3 no (0,d)). So only (2,0,2,1).\n- a=3: b=1 => c=3 => d∈{2,1} but only d=2 works (d=1 fails c4). So (3,1,3,2) valid.\n\nThus left assignments are exactly those five we listed.\n\nThus only L5 yields a valid full solution.\n\nThus the right component enumeration is just the tuple (2,3,1,0). However, we need to confirm that (2,3,1,0) indeed satisfies c5-c8 and cross constraints. Yes.\n\nThus the answer_requirements: \"Give one complete assignment for a through h satisfying all ten constraints.\" So we can give the assignment (a=3,b=1,c=3,d=2,e=2,f=3,g=1,h=0). That's one solution.\n\n\"Enumerate every satisfying tuple for your assigned component, in variable order.\" The assigned component is the right component (e,f,g,h). So we need to enumerate all satisfying tuples for (e,f,g,h). That is just (2,3,1,0). So we need to output that enumeration.\n\nNow we need to produce a JSON protocol envelope matching the supplied template. The envelope template includes fields: intent, message_id, payload.report, problem_id, read_refs, recipients, reply_to, schema_version, sender.\n\nWe need to fill in the envelope with appropriate values. The envelope is for reporting evidence. The left-reviewer is checking the right component tuple enumeration. The left-reviewer is to report that the enumeration is supported (i.e., correct). So we need to set assessment to \"supported\". The basis_refs should reference the constraints that support the enumeration. Likely all constraints c1-c10. The target is the problem resource. The read_refs also reference the problem. The recipients is \"shared-integrator\". The reply_to is null. The schema_version is 1. The sender is \"left-reviewer\". The message_id is \"csp-5103-left-reviewer-1\". That matches the template.\n\nThus we need to output exactly that envelope, perhaps with additional fields for the enumeration? The envelope template does not have a field for enumeration. But the role task says \"Edit assessment, basis_refs, and candidate tuples only.\" So there is a \"candidate tuples\" field that we need to edit. However, the envelope template does not show it. Possibly the envelope template is incomplete, and we need to add a \"candidate_tuples\" field inside \"payload.report\". The user says \"Edit assessment, basis_refs, and candidate tuples only.\" So we need to include candidate tuples. The envelope template likely expects a \"candidate_tuples\" field inside \"payload.report\". But it's not shown. However, the envelope template is given as a JSON snippet. It includes \"payload\": {\"report\": {...}}. The \"report\" currently includes \"assessment\", \"basis_refs\", \"target\". It doesn't include \"candidate_tuples\". But the user says we can edit candidate tuples. So maybe we need to add a \"candidate_tuples\" field inside \"report\". That would be editing beyond the template, but the user says we can edit candidate tuples. So we can add a \"candidate_tuples\" field.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the envelope template is the final answer, and we just need to output it as is, because the left-reviewer is reporting that the enumeration is supported. The candidate tuples might be provided elsewhere (maybe in a separate message). But the user says \"Independently check the right component tuple enumeration against the public constraints.\" So we need to check the enumeration. But we have no enumeration provided. So maybe the enumeration is the solution we need to provide. So we need to produce a report that includes the enumeration. The envelope template is a skeleton; we need to fill in the candidate tuples.\n\nThus I think we need to produce an envelope that includes the assignment and enumeration. However, the envelope template does not have fields for assignment or enumeration. But maybe the \"payload.report\" can contain any fields, not just assessment, basis_refs, target. The template shows those fields, but we can add more fields. The user says \"Edit assessment, basis_refs, and candidate tuples only.\" So we can add candidate tuples, but we cannot edit other fields (like target). So we can add a \"candidate_tuples\" field inside \"payload.report\". That would be the enumeration.\n\nThus we need to produce a JSON envelope that matches the template structure but includes a \"candidate_tuples\" field. The envelope template is a JSON object with specific fields. We can add extra fields as long as we keep the required fields. The user says \"matching the supplied template\". That could be interpreted as the envelope must have the same fields as the template (maybe plus extra). But it's ambiguous. Usually \"matching the supplied template\" means the JSON should follow the same structure, but we can fill in values. It doesn't necessarily forbid adding extra fields. However, the user also says \"Edit assessment, basis_refs, and candidate tuples only.\" That suggests we can edit those fields (maybe they exist). But we can also add candidate tuples if not present. The envelope currently does not have candidate tuples. So we need to add them.\n\nThus we need to produce an envelope with the same fields as the template, but we can add a \"candidate_tuples\" field inside \"payload.report\". Also we can edit assessment and basis_refs if needed. But we can keep them as is (supported, list of constraints). So we can keep assessment \"supported\", basis_refs list of all constraints. Then add \"candidate_tuples\": [[2,3,1,0]] (or maybe a list of tuples). The enumeration is a list of tuples for the right component. The variable order is e,f,g,h. So the tuple is [2,3,1,0].\n\nThus the envelope would be something like:\n\n{\n  \"intent\": \"REPORT_EVIDENCE\",\n  \"message_id\": \"csp-5103-left-reviewer-1\",\n  \"payload\": {\n    \"report\": {\n      \"assessment\": \"supported\",\n      \"basis_refs\": [\"c1\",\"c2\",\"c3\",\"c4\",\"c5\",\"c6\",\"c7\",\"c8\",\"c9\",\"c10\"],\n      \"target\": {\n        \"resource_id\": \"problem:csp-5103\",\n        \"revision\": 1,\n        \"state_revision\": 1\n      },\n      \"candidate_tuples\": [[2,3,1,0]]\n    }\n  },\n  \"problem_id\": \"csp-5103\",\n  \"read_refs\": [\n    {\n      \"resource_id\": \"problem:csp-5103\",\n      \"revision\": 1,\n      \"state_revision\": 1\n    }\n  ],\n  \"recipients\": [\n    \"shared-integrator\"\n  ],\n  \"reply_to\": null,\n  \"schema_version\": 1,\n  \"sender\": \"left-reviewer\"\n}\n\nBut we also need to provide the complete assignment for a through h. The answer_requirements says \"Give one complete assignment for a through h satisfying all ten constraints.\" That is part of the solution. However, the envelope is a report about the right component enumeration. It might not include the assignment. But the user says \"Solve the finite constraint task from the complete immutable public packet below. Return exactly one JSON protocol envelope matching the supplied template; no prose outside JSON.\" So maybe the envelope is the solution. The envelope includes the assignment and enumeration. But the envelope template does not have fields for assignment. However, maybe the assignment is considered a \"candidate tuple\"? But candidate tuples are for the right component. The left component assignment is not part of candidate tuples. But maybe we need to include the assignment as a separate field, like \"assignment\": {\"a\":3,...}. But the envelope template does not have that. The user says \"Edit assessment, basis_refs, and candidate tuples only.\" So we cannot add assignment field. So maybe the assignment is not required in the envelope; 